  <p class="leg">Schematic map. The size of each dot is proportional to the number of
  ancestors born in that commune. Nantes and Ancenis are shown as landmarks;
  Couëron, in red, will reappear later.</p>
  <div class="grid2" style="margin-top:56px">
    <div>
      <p>This concentration is not incidental. It tells the story of a society where
      one married one's neighbour, where the land was never left, and where the same
      surnames return generation after generation. The parish records bear direct
      witness to it: Pierre Ouary and Anne Ouarie, married around 1730, were cousins.
      The whole line of descent therefore traces back twice to the same
      sixteenth-century ancestor, by two distinct paths.</p>
      <p>Two exceptions break this circle. The Vavasseur, blacksmiths, came from the
      borderlands of Morbihan and Côtes-d'Armor, a hundred and fifty kilometres away.
      And one branch left for America — the subject of Part Six.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top:44px">What people lived on</h3>
      <ul class="barres" style="max-width:520px"><li><span class="b"><em>Laboureur</em><i style="width:100.0%"></i></span><span class="v">22</span></li><li><span class="b">Farmer (f.)<i style="width:36.4%"></i></span><span class="v">8</span></li><li><span class="b">Farmer (m.)<i style="width:18.2%"></i></span><span class="v">4</span></li><li><span class="b">Blacksmith<i style="width:13.6%"></i></span><span class="v">3</span></li><li><span class="b">Baker<i style="width:9.1%"></i></span><span class="v">2</span></li><li><span class="b">Innkeeper<i style="width:9.1%"></i></span><span class="v">2</span></li><li><span class="b">Mason<i style="width:9.1%"></i></span><span class="v">2</span></li><li><span class="b">Sharecropper<i style="width:4.5%"></i></span><span class="v">1</span></li><li><span class="b">Carpenter<i style="width:4.5%"></i></span><span class="v">1</span></li><li><span class="b">Housewife<i style="width:4.5%"></i></span><span class="v">1</span></li></ul>
      <p class="leg">Occupations recorded for direct ancestors only. A
      <em>laboureur</em> was not a day labourer: he was the peasant who owned his own
      plough team and ploughs — the top of the village hierarchy.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Communes of origin</h3>
      <ul class="barres"><li><span class="b">Mésanger<i style="width:100.0%"></i></span><span class="v">56</span></li><li><span class="b">Les Touches<i style="width:66.1%"></i></span><span class="v">37</span></li><li><span class="b">Carquefou<i style="width:44.6%"></i></span><span class="v">25</span></li><li><span class="b">Ligné<i style="width:28.6%"></i></span><span class="v">16</span></li><li><span class="b">Riaillé<i style="width:28.6%"></i></span><span class="v">16</span></li><li><span class="b">Petit-Mars<i style="width:21.4%"></i></span><span class="v">12</span></li><li><span class="b">Saint-Herblon<i style="width:19.6%"></i></span><span class="v">11</span></li><li><span class="b">Teillé<i style="width:12.5%"></i></span><span class="v">7</span></li><li><span class="b">Bonnœuvre<i style="width:10.7%"></i></span><span class="v">6</span></li><li><span class="b">Grand-Auverné<i style="width:7.1%"></i></span><span class="v">4</span></li><li><span class="b">Pouillé-les-Côteaux<i style="width:7.1%"></i></span><span class="v">4</span></li></ul>
