      <div class="nuage"><span>OUARY <b>15</b></span><span>BONNET <b>9</b></span><span>MULOISE <b>9</b></span><span>DUTERTRE <b>9</b></span><span>JOURDON <b>8</b></span><span>GODARD <b>8</b></span><span>LEFEUVRE <b>8</b></span><span>LEDUC <b>7</b></span><span>SEBILLEAU <b>7</b></span><span>DAVODEAU <b>7</b></span><span>BLANCHET <b>6</b></span><span>LERAY <b>6</b></span><span>DELANOUE <b>6</b></span><span>RIGAUD <b>6</b></span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="num-sec">II · Three lives</div>
  <h2>Leaving the anonymity<br>of the registers</h2>
  <p class="chapo">A peasant ancestor generally leaves only three lines: a baptism, a
  marriage, a burial. Three men in this line are the exception, because an event threw
  them into the archives — a civil war, an industry, a photograph.</p>

  <div class="vies">
    <article class="vie">
      <figure class="ph "><img src="/img/genealogie/linsurrection-des-chouans-jean-sorieul.jpg" alt="The Chouan insurrection, by Jean Sorieul"><figcaption>A column of Chouans in ambush. No portrait of Jacques Guérin exists: nineteenth-century painting remembered the other side instead. <span class="cr">Jean Sorieul, 19th century, public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%E2%80%99Insurrection_des_chouans_%28Jean_Sorieul%29.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure>
      <div class="rang">7th generation</div>
      <h3>Jacques Guérin</h3>
      <div class="dates">c. 1747 — 7 June 1794</div>
      <div class="role">baker, innkeeper, municipal officer</div>
      <p>He kept a bakery and the inn of <em>L'Écu de France</em> in Riaillé, and served
      the commune as municipal officer. On the night of 6 to 7 June 1794 — 19 Prairial
      Year II in the French Republican calendar — the <em>chouans</em> (royalist
      insurgents of the Chouannerie, the counter-revolutionary uprising in western
      France) from the Segré area, led by Sarrazin, raided Loire-Inférieure: they sacked
      the forges and massacred some thirty republicans. Jacques Guérin was killed in the
      back room of his bakery, at the top of the town.</p>
      <p>The register records him as the widower of Jacquette Letort, aged
      forty-seven. His brother-in-law Jacques Letort, mayor of Saint-Mars-la-Jaille, had
      been shot by the chouans six months earlier. The site of his death later became
      the Café Delanoue — named after his own descendants.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="vie">
      <figure class="ph "><img src="/img/genealogie/bretagne-les-forges-des-salles-1671.jpg" alt="The ironworking village of Forges des Salles" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Les Forges des Salles, in the Quénécan forest. Manor house, workers' terraces, charcoal halls: the village has not changed since 1877. <span class="cr">Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bretagne%2C_Les_Forges_des_Salles-1671.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure>
      <div class="rang">9th generation</div>
      <h3>François Vavasseur</h3>
      <div class="dates">1699 — 1762</div>
      <div class="role">blacksmith at Forges des Salles</div>
      <p>The only non-peasant branch in the whole ancestry. He married Marie Le Breton
      at Forges des Salles on 9 February 1728 and had twelve children with her, all
      born in this village lost deep in the Quénécan forest. At least five would not
      reach adulthood.</p>
      <p>He died there in 1762, aged sixty-three. His father Olivier had already been a
      blacksmith, his son-in-law Jacques Chauvel was a hammerman: three iron trades
      under the same roof. The rest of this story fills the next section.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="vie">
      <figure class="ph portrait"><img src="/img/genealogie/louis-frederic-bonnet.jpg" alt="Louis Frédéric Bonnet in stretcher-bearer's uniform, 1914-1918" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Louis Frédéric Bonnet in uniform, armband on his sleeve, during the Great War. <span class="cr">Family collection</span></figcaption></figure>
      <div class="rang">3rd generation</div>
      <h3>Louis Frédéric Bonnet</h3>
      <div class="dates">1884 — 1957</div>
      <div class="role">stretcher-bearer, farmhand, farmer</div>
      <p>A stretcher-bearer during the Great War. His brother Henri was killed there in
      1915, aged twenty-nine, in the attack at Quennevières. A photograph shows him in
      uniform, armband on his sleeve.</p>
      <p>Before farming a very small holding at Carquefou, he was employed — a
      "valet," as the family called it — by the Marquis de Dion, probably at the
      Château de Maubreuil. The same man who founded the De Dion-Bouton works, for a
      time the world's leading car manufacturer. The gap between the two destinies,
      within the same field of view, says a good deal about what rural society was
      like in 1910.</p>
    </article>
  </div>


  <blockquote>
    We, René Chollet, public officer appointed for the execution of the law of
    20 September 1792 in the territory of the commune of Riaillé, department of
    Loire-Inférieure, district of Ancenis, upon the declaration made to us by the
    widows of those named below, murdered by the Brigands who came to the town on
    Saturday 29 Prairial and there slaughtered:
    <cite>Civil register of Riaillé, 19 Prairial Year II</cite>
  </blockquote>
  <ul class="morts" style="margin-top:0;border-top:0"><li><span class="n">Mathieu Desvallets</span><span class="a">28</span><span class="l">gendarme · husband of Marie Leconte</span></li><li><span class="n">Louis Bechu</span><span class="a">35</span><span class="l">gendarme · husband of Marie Guillois</span></li><li><span class="n">Julien Quenelle</span><span class="a">46</span><span class="l">gendarme · husband of Marie Guais</span></li><li><span class="n">Louis Jean</span><span class="a">35</span><span class="l">gendarme</span></li><li><span class="n">Étienne Blanchard</span><span class="a">45</span><span class="l">merchant · husband of Marie Coudoux</span></li><li><span class="n">Jacques Guérin</span><span class="a">47</span><span class="l">baker, innkeeper and municipal officer · widower of Jacquette Letort</span></li></ul>

  <div class="grid2" style="margin-top:52px">
    <div>
      <p>There are six of them. The massacre claimed about thirty. The discrepancy is
      not a contradiction: the public officer only recorded the deaths of those whose
      <strong>widows came forward to make a declaration</strong>. The unmarried, the
      widowers, those whose family never came forward, appear nowhere. Jacques Guérin
      is there because he was a widower himself, and his descendants had his death
      recorded in the civil register.</p>
      <p>There follows a long list of residents who came to testify, each with his
      age, occupation and wife's name: <em>laboureurs à bras</em> (labourers who worked
      the land by hand, without a plough team — poorer than the ordinary laboureur), a
      day labourer, a mason, a cattle merchant, and two innkeepers from the town of
      Saint-Mars-la-Jaille — the same trade as the dead man.</p>
      <p>One last copying detail. The register reads "Saturday 29 Prairial," but
      29 Prairial Year II fell on a Tuesday. The 19th, however, was indeed a Saturday:
      the copyist mistook one digit for another.</p>
    </div>
    <div><figure class="ph haut"><img src="/img/genealogie/proces-verbal-riaille-1794.jpg" alt="Official report of the Riaillé massacre, 19 Prairial Year II" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>The rest of the official report: the witnesses summoned, with their age, occupation and wife's name. <span class="cr">Family collection</span></figcaption></figure></div>
  </div>
</section>


<section>
  <div class="num-sec">III · The road of iron</div>
  <h2>Two hundred and forty-eight kilometres,<br>six generations</h2>
  <p class="chapo">Breton ironworkers were not settled folk. A charcoal-fired
  ironworks devours its forest: when the charcoal runs short, the blast furnace is
  banked while the coppice regrows, and the families move on to the next site. The
  Vavasseur, and then the Chauvel they married into, followed the iron this way for
  two centuries — from the forest of Paimpont to the <em>bocage</em> (the hedgerow
  farmland landscape typical of western France) of Riaillé.</p>
  <div class="viz"><svg viewBox="0 0 940 520" role="img" aria-label="Map of the ironworks route">
<polyline class="fer-l" points="484.4,207.4 446.1,130.0 245.0,137.6 651.4,301.3 695.0,390.0"/>
<g transform="translate(34,492)"><line x1="0" y1="0" x2="66.4" y2="0" class="sc"/><line x1="0" y1="-5" x2="0" y2="5" class="sc"/><line x1="66.4" y1="-5" x2="66.4" y2="5" class="sc"/><text x="33.2" y="-11" class="sc-t" text-anchor="middle">20 km</text></g>
<circle cx="484.4" cy="207.4" r="9" class="fer-h"/>
<circle cx="484.4" cy="207.4" r="4.5" class="fer-p"/>
<text x="468.4" y="195.4" class="fer-an" text-anchor="end">1663</text>
