<circle cx="695.0" cy="390.0" r="9" class="fer-h"/>
<circle cx="695.0" cy="390.0" r="4.5" class="fer-p"/>
<text x="679.0" y="378.0" class="fer-an" text-anchor="end">1875</text>
<text x="679.0" y="394.0" class="fer-n" text-anchor="end">Riaillé</text>
<text x="679.0" y="409.0" class="fer-t" text-anchor="end">The forge closes</text>
</svg></div>
  <p class="leg">Schematic map. The dates correspond to the family event attested at
  each site.</p>

  <div class="grid2" style="margin-top:52px">
    <div>
      <p>The route is anything but aimless wandering. Each of these stops is a major
      ironworking site, and all belong to the same world: that of the charcoal-fired
      ironworks of inland Brittany, where the ore surfaces and the forest supplies the
      fuel. Paimpont and its eight thousand hectares, la Hardouinais mentioned as
      early as 1570, Quénécan and its three thousand six hundred hectares.</p>
      <p>What is striking about these trajectories is that they were the rule.
      Genealogists of the region have long since identified these families
      circulating between the Paimpont, la Hardouinais, Loudéac and Quénécan forest
      massifs, crossing the boundaries of Ille-et-Vilaine, Côtes-d'Armor and Morbihan
      before moving down into Loire-Atlantique — Héric, Orvault, Sautron and,
      precisely, Sion-les-Mines. The Vavasseur are a textbook case.</p>
      <p>The trade itself broke down further: <em>fondeur</em> (furnace smelter),
      <em>affineur</em> (iron refiner), <em>marteleur</em> (hammerman),
      <em>chauffeur</em> (furnace stoker), <em>valet de bordeur</em> (an edger's
      assistant). Jacques Chauvel, who married Anne Vavasseur, was a marteleur — the
      one who beat the iron bloom under the hydraulic trip hammer. Forge families
      married among themselves, and the position passed down along with the name.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p>The last stop closes the loop in a way that chance only half explains. In
      1875, the Riaillé forges shut down for good. A family note, taken from a
      website devoted to the history of the bocage's ironworks, records that their
      last director was named Chauvel. Olivier Chauvel, a blacksmith turned innkeeper
      and tavern-keeper, great-grandson of François Vavasseur, died at Riaillé on
      14 March of that same year, 1875.</p>
      <p>The file does not establish that this director was him: his declared
      occupation remains "blacksmith, innkeeper." But the coincidence of place, name
      and year is worth noting, and would deserve checking against the archives.</p>
      <p>Riaillé is also the commune where, eighty years earlier, the chouans had
      sacked the forges and killed Jacques Guérin. Two branches of the same
      ancestry, one coming from the bocage and the other from the forests of central
      Brittany, meet in the same industrial village.</p>
      <p>Riaillé did not in fact have a single forge but three sites staggered along
      the same waterway: the blast furnace at la Poitevinière, the finery forge
      downstream at la Provostière, a slitting mill at the far end at la Vallée. The
      two ponds, seventy-five and seventy-three hectares, were the largest in the
      region. They cast nails, cauldrons, fireplace backs and cannonballs there.</p>
      <p>The blast furnace at la Poitevinière still stands: eight metres high by
      nine on a side, built of schist, with a circular hearth. Listed as a historic
      monument (<em>Monument historique</em>) on 1 April 1986, it is the last
      survivor of the Pays de la Mée's metallurgical adventure. The place's name
      comes from the families brought from Poitou to work the ore — another
      migration of iron, four centuries before that of the Vavasseur.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="photos" style="margin-top:44px">
    <figure class="ph wide-img"><img src="/img/genealogie/bretagne-les-forges-des-salles-1691.jpg" alt="Les Forges des Salles, village buildings" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>The village has not changed since the blast furnace stopped, on 1 July 1877. <span class="cr">Wikimedia Commons · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bretagne%2C_Les_Forges_des_Salles-1691.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="ph wide-img"><img src="/img/genealogie/bretagne-les-forges-des-salles-1673.jpg" alt="Les Forges des Salles, village buildings" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Founded in 1623 by the Rohan family, the site produced iron for two hundred and fifty years. <span class="cr">Wikimedia Commons · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bretagne%2C_Les_Forges_des_Salles-1673.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="ph wide-img"><img src="/img/genealogie/bretagne-les-forges-des-salles-1676.jpg" alt="Les Forges des Salles, village buildings" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>The ironmaster's manor house, workers' terraces, canteen, school, chapel, terraced gardens: listed as a historic monument in 1980, the whole site can be visited. <span class="cr">Wikimedia Commons · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bretagne%2C_Les_Forges_des_Salles-1676.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:78px;font-size:26px">At journey's end: la Poitevinière</h3>
  <div class="grid2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div>
      <p>The hamlet of la Poitevinière, at Riaillé, where this two-century migration
      comes to an end. The same pond powered both the forge's bellows and the mill's
      grindstones: when the blast furnace went cold, the flour mill kept turning at
      the foot of its chimney.</p>
      <p>It is this chimney that can still be seen, rising above the hamlet's
      rooftops, ringed in iron and strapped with a ladder. It is all that remains
      standing of the works that supported the Chauvel for three generations, and
      the last witness to the Pays de la Mée's metallurgical adventure.</p>
      <p>One more detail belongs here, unrelated by blood but telling of how dense
      these places were: the house next to the blast furnace is the one where the
      ancestors of Sophie Trébuchet lived — the mother of Victor Hugo.</p>
    </div>
    <div><figure class="ph haut"><img src="/img/genealogie/poiteviniere-cheminee.jpg" alt="The blast furnace chimney at la Poitevinière" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>The blast furnace chimney, listed as a historic monument on 1 April 1986. <span class="cr">chouannerie.chez-alice.fr</span></figcaption></figure></div>
  </div>
  <div class="photos" style="margin-top:32px">
    <figure class="ph wide-img"><img src="/img/genealogie/poiteviniere-1.jpg" alt="The flour mill at la Poitevinière and the blast furnace chimney" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>The flour mill and, behind it, the blast furnace chimney. <span class="cr">chouannerie.chez-alice.fr</span></figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="ph wide-img"><img src="/img/genealogie/poiteviniere-2.jpg" alt="Interior of the flour mill at la Poitevinière" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Inside the flour mill: pulleys and drive belts, still in place beneath the floor. <span class="cr">chouannerie.chez-alice.fr</span></figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="ph wide-img"><img src="/img/genealogie/poiteviniere-3.jpg" alt="The clerk's house, at la Poitevinière" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>The clerk's house. He was the one who kept the forge's accounts, weighed the iron and paid the charcoal-burners — the go-between for the often-absent ironmaster and the workers. <span class="cr">chouannerie.chez-alice.fr</span></figcaption></figure>
  </div>
</section>


<section>
  <div class="num-sec">IV · La Rigaudière</div>
  <h2>The inn, the forge<br>and the workshop</h2>
  <p class="chapo">A hamlet of Riaillé, and four members of the same kinship group
  working there at the same time. Auguste Juvin and his wife Marie Rosalie Ouary ran
  the hotel-restaurant there. Her brother, Olivier Ouary, was a blacksmith there.
  Their son-in-law Joseph Muloise, who married their daughter Marguerite, was a
  carpenter there. The drinks trade, the forge and the woodworking shop, all in the
  same hamlet, in the hands of a single family, across two generations.</p>
  <p style="margin-top:24px">The detail is not incidental: it extends the previous
  story exactly. The Chauvel had followed the iron all the way to Riaillé, where the
  forge went cold in 1875. The trade itself did not die out — it retreated from the
  blast furnace to the village anvil, and it was an Ouary who held it at la
  Rigaudière a generation later.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:64px;font-size:22px;font-weight:600">Five generations behind a counter</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:14px">Running an inn was never a trade on its own. It always
  came second, backed by another occupation: one was a baker <em>and</em> innkeeper,
  a blacksmith <em>and</em> tavern-keeper, a carpenter <em>and</em> café-keeper. In a
  bocage market town, the drinks trade was the supplementary income, and the place
  everyone passed through.</p>
  <ul class="metiers"><li><span class="p"><b>Jacques Guérin</b><span>c. 1747 – 1794 · 7<sup>th</sup> generation</span></span><span class="m">baker</span><span class="a">innkeeper of <em>L'Écu de France</em>, Riaillé</span></li><li><span class="p"><b>Olivier Chauvel</b><span>1799 – 1875 · 6<sup>th</sup> generation</span></span><span class="m">blacksmith</span><span class="a">innkeeper and tavern-keeper, Sion-les-Mines then Riaillé</span></li><li><span class="p"><b>Anne Marie Morel</b><span>1806 – ? · 6<sup>th</sup> generation</span></span><span class="m">—</span><span class="a">innkeeper, Sion-les-Mines</span></li><li><span class="p"><b>Rosalie Richard</b><span>? – 1902 · 4<sup>th</sup> generation</span></span><span class="m">—</span><span class="a">innkeeper</span></li><li><span class="p"><b>Auguste Juvin</b><span>1855 – 1915 · 4<sup>th</sup> generation</span></span><span class="m">—</span><span class="a">café-keeper and hotelier, la Rigaudière</span></li><li><span class="p"><b>Marie Rosalie Ouary</b><span>1855 – 1939 · 4<sup>th</sup> generation</span></span><span class="m">—</span><span class="a">hotel-restaurant at la Rigaudière</span></li><li><span class="p"><b>Joseph Muloise</b><span>1891 – 1945 · 3<sup>rd</sup> generation</span></span><span class="m">carpenter</span><span class="a">café-keeper, la Rigaudière</span></li><li><span class="p"><b>René Blanchet</b><span>1893 – 1964 · 3<sup>rd</sup> generation</span></span><span class="m">carpenter</span><span class="a">café-keeper, Teillé</span></li></ul>
  <p style="margin-top:26px">This sheds light, in passing, on a detail from Part Two.
  If Jacques Guérin was municipal officer in 1794, that was no accident of
  temperament: he kept the town's inn. In a commune of a thousand inhabitants, the
  innkeeper sees the news, the pedlars and the gendarmes go by. That is also what
  makes him a target: the chouans knew where to find him.</p>

  <div class="reserve" style="margin-top:44px">
    <h3>What the declared occupation doesn't say</h3>
    <p>These occupations come from civil-registration records, where each person
    declares themselves as they see fit. The same man could be a "laboureur" at his
    marriage and an "innkeeper" at his third child's baptism, without having changed
    his life: he simply mentioned the other half of what he did. The occupations
    recorded here are therefore snapshots, not careers.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:64px;font-size:22px;font-weight:600">Then no one at all</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:14px">Among the four hundred and twenty-six reconstructed
  ancestors, there are twenty-two laboureurs, farmers, blacksmiths, carpenters,
  masons, a wool carder, a miller, a handful of innkeepers — and exactly two
  occupations that resemble no other, both at the first generation. A history
  teacher and journalist; a natural-science teacher.</p>
  <p>Four centuries of land, iron and the counter, and then the first generation to
  teach. What sixteen generations had not shifted tips over in just one.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="num-sec">V · 1914-1918</div>
  <h2>Those who died,<br>the one who wrote</h2>
  <p class="chapo">Two men of this ancestry died at the front. Neither is a direct
  ancestor, and that is no coincidence: a line is only passed on by those who came
  back. They are two brothers of ancestors — the great-grandfather's, and the
  great-great-grandmother's.</p>
  <div class="viz"><svg viewBox="0 0 940 300" role="img" aria-label="The five deaths of 1914-1918">
<line x1="66" y1="150" x2="874" y2="150" class="fr-axe"/>
<line x1="119.9" y1="143" x2="119.9" y2="157" class="fr-tick"/>
<text x="119.9" y="176" class="fr-an" text-anchor="middle">1915</text>
<line x1="658.5" y1="143" x2="658.5" y2="157" class="fr-tick"/>
<text x="658.5" y="176" class="fr-an" text-anchor="middle">1916</text>
<line x1="351.5" y1="150" x2="351.5" y2="110" class="fr-tige"/>
<circle cx="351.5" cy="150" r="6" class="fr-p"/>
<text x="351.5" y="76.0" class="fr-n" text-anchor="middle">Henri Bonnet</text>
<text x="351.5" y="92.0" class="fr-t" text-anchor="middle">29 · 6 June 1915</text>
<text x="351.5" y="108.0" class="fr-t2" text-anchor="middle">Quennevières, Oise</text>
<line x1="701.6" y1="150" x2="701.6" y2="190" class="fr-tige"/>
<circle cx="701.6" cy="150" r="6" class="fr-p"/>
<text x="701.6" y="204.0" class="fr-n" text-anchor="middle">Jean Jumois</text>
<text x="701.6" y="220.0" class="fr-t" text-anchor="middle">33 · 31 Jan. 1916</text>
<text x="701.6" y="236.0" class="fr-t2" text-anchor="middle">Tahure, Marne</text>
</svg></div>
  <ul class="morts" style="margin-top:44px"><li><span class="n">Henri Auguste Bonnet</span><span class="a">29 · 1915</span><span class="l">Quennevières, Oise</span></li><li><span class="n">Jean Marie Joseph Jumois</span><span class="a">33 · 1916</span><span class="l">Tahure, Marne</span></li></ul>
  <p style="margin-top:26px">Henri Bonnet and Louis Frédéric, the great-grandfather,
  were brothers, born a year apart. Both went to war; one came back, married Marie
  Bernardeau in 1924 and farmed a holding at Carquefou until 1957. The other fell at
  twenty-nine at Quennevières, a farm name in the Oise that in June 1915 came to
  designate an entire offensive. Eight months later, Jean Jumois died at Tahure, in
  Champagne.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:78px;font-size:26px">The letters of René Blanchet</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:14px">Both great-grandfathers of this line went to war and
  came back. Louis Frédéric Bonnet, a stretcher-bearer, on the paternal side. René
  Joseph Blanchet, born at Teillé on 20 March 1893, on the maternal side — in the
  114<sup>th</sup> Infantry Regiment, 2<sup>nd</sup> Company, postal sector 166. Of
  him, the family has kept letters, written almost every week to his father, a
  carpenter at Teillé, and numbered so that none would be lost.</p>
  <p>They do not tell of battles. They speak of the price of wine — thirty-six to
  forty <em>sous</em> a litre, "far too expensive for soldiers" —, the cinema and
  the singers of an evening at billets, the mud one walks through by day, and the
  happiness of sleeping through a whole night. On 20 January 1918, the regiment is
  resting near Blainville, in Meurthe-et-Moselle: it has just left the Forêt de
  Parroy, where it had held a quiet sector for five months.</p>

  <div class="photos" style="margin-top:40px">
    <figure class="ph wide-img"><img src="/img/genealogie/lettre-1918-01-20.jpg" alt="Letter from René Blanchet, 20 January 1918" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Sunday 20 January 1918, letter no. 4. "My dear papa." <span class="cr">Family collection</span></figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="ph wide-img"><img src="/img/genealogie/lettre-1918-01-27.jpg" alt="Letter from René Blanchet, 27 January 1918" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Sunday 27 January 1918, letter no. 6, with its postmark. <span class="cr">Family collection</span></figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="ph wide-img"><img src="/img/genealogie/lettre-1918-02-21.jpg" alt="Letter from René Blanchet, 21 February 1918" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>21 February 1918: the account of an attack he did not take part in. <span class="cr">Family collection</span></figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <div class="grid2" style="margin-top:52px">
    <div>
      <p>Then comes June 1918. The 114<sup>th</sup> had left Lorraine at Easter for
      the Somme, and found itself engaged at Méry, in the Oise, facing the German
      offensive. For three days — the 11th, 12th and 13th of June — the regiment
      held; the 650 men it lost there, including twenty-four officers, would earn it
      the <em>Croix de guerre</em>. Mangin would sum up the affair in a line about
      "the unforgettable green of the wheat on 11 June, and the red, that of the
      blood that stained it."</p>
      <p>René Blanchet was one of them. Wounded in the left side on the 12th,
      evacuated, he crossed Paris by car and wrote on Friday 14 June from Auxiliary
      Hospital No. 3 at Fleury-Meudon. His letter is mostly concerned with
      reassurance: the wound is less serious than he thought, it's nothing now, and
      above all his father should not come — he might not be there any more.</p>
      <p>One line, in passing, reveals the scale of what the family did not know:
      eight hundred wounded arriving in Paris within forty-eight hours, in wards set
      up in haste, and it went on like that the whole time.</p>
    </div>
    <div><figure class="ph haut"><img src="/img/genealogie/lettre-1918-06-14.jpg" alt="Letter of 14 June 1918, written from the hospital" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Friday 14 June 1918, written from Auxiliary Hospital No. 3 at Fleury-Meudon, two days after he was wounded. <span class="cr">Family collection</span></figcaption></figure></div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<div class="aud">
<div class="wide">
<section style="border-top:0">
  <div class="num-sec">VI · The American cousin</div>
  <h2>John James Audubon</h2>
  <p class="chapo">The world's most famous painter of birds was born Jean Rabin, on
  26 April 1785 at Les Cayes, in Saint-Domingue. His mother, Jeanne Rabine, had been
  born twenty-seven years earlier in the hamlet of Les Mazures, commune of Les
  Touches — in the very parish where the Ouary had been laboureurs for two
  centuries. Both lines trace back to the same man.</p>

  <div class="photos" style="margin-top:44px">
    <figure class="ph "><img src="/img/genealogie/john-james-audubon-bird-naturalist-lccn2002700147.jpg" alt="Portrait of John James Audubon" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>John James Audubon. The naturalist posed as a trapper to serve his American legend. <span class="cr">Library of Congress, public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_James_Audubon%2C_bird_naturalist_LCCN2002700147.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="ph "><img src="/img/genealogie/audubonjj.jpg" alt="John James Audubon" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Born Jean Rabin at Les Cayes, died John James Audubon in Manhattan: three names for one life. <span class="cr">Wikimedia Commons, public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AudubonJJ.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="ph "><img src="/img/genealogie/john-james-audubon-1785-1851-head-and-shoulders-portrait-fac.jpg" alt="Memorial portrait of Audubon surrounded by bird vignettes" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Memorial portrait, surrounded by vignettes drawn from his plates. <span class="cr">Library of Congress, public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_James_Audubon%2C_1785-1851%2C_head-and-shoulders_portrait%2C_facing_right_in_oval_memorial_portrait_fringed_by_bird_vignettes_LCCN2005689323.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:78px;font-size:26px">The two branches</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:14px">Guillaume Ouary, a laboureur at Les Touches, died in
  1652. Two of his children opened two unrelated stories: his son Pierre stayed in
  the village, his daughter Renée was the origin of the line that would lead to
  Audubon. Eleven generations on one side, seven on the other.</p>
