  <p class="leg">Sixth-degree cousinship, four generations apart.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:78px;font-size:26px">Jeanne Rabine, lady's maid</h3>
  <div class="grid2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div>
      <p>Baptised at Les Touches, at the church of Sainte-Mélaine, on 14 March
      1758. Cébron the tanner, a neighbour from the hamlet of Les Mazures,
      recommended her to Jacques Pallon de la Bouverie, a retired judge turned
      planter. She embarked at the Quai de la Fosse in Nantes aboard <em>Le
      Conquérant</em>, with the Pallon family, for Saint-Domingue. She was a little
      over twenty, and would never see Brittany again.</p>
      <p>The date of this departure is not settled. The commune of Les Touches
      gives 23 October 1781; Yvon Chatelain's biography, repeated by the
      genealogical databases, states September 1783. Two years apart between two
      secondhand sources, which no archival document consulted here can resolve.
      The ships' muster rolls held at the Archives départementales de
      Loire-Atlantique would settle it.</p>
      <p>The ship itself cannot be traced. The name recurred often among Nantes
      shipowners — a <em>Conquérant</em> of Nantes appears as early as 1746 among
      the prizes seized by the English — and nothing allows Jeanne Rabine's ship to
      be identified. One thing is certain, however: carrying a magistrate, his
      wife, his three daughters and their servant, it sailed <em>en droiture</em>,
      the direct route to the colony, rather than the triangular circuit of the
      slave trade. A nuance that matters, since the man who took her in on arrival
      was himself a slave-ship captain.</p>
      <p>At Les Cayes, she met Jean Audubon, a Nantes sea captain, planter and
      slave trader, married in France. She died a few months after her son's
      birth, carried off by a tropical fever. The child was entrusted to Catherine
      Bouffard, his father's companion, then brought back to France in 1788.</p>
      <p>He grew up at La Gerbetière, in Couëron, with his father's lawful wife.
      In 1794, the couple adopted him under the first name <em>Fougère</em> — the
      name of the day of his birth in the French Republican calendar. In 1803,
      equipped with a false passport meant to spare him conscription, he sailed
      for the United States under the name John James Audubon.</p>
    </div>
    <div><figure class="ph "><img src="/img/genealogie/w1318-touches-stemelaine-xviii1849-1861-1895-51530.jpg" alt="Sainte-Mélaine church at Les Touches" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>The Sainte-Mélaine church at Les Touches, the parish where Jeanne Rabine was baptised in 1758. <span class="cr">Llann Wé², CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:W1318-Touches_SteMelaine_XVIII%C2%B01849-1861-1895_51530.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure></div>
  </div>
