  <h3 style="margin-top:78px;font-size:26px">A life</h3>
  <ul class="chrono"><li><span class="an">1785</span><span class="tx">Born at Les Cayes, in Saint-Domingue, under the name Jean Rabin. His mother dies a few months later.</span></li><li><span class="an">1788</span><span class="tx">His father brings him to France. He grows up at La Gerbetière, in Couëron, with his father's lawful wife.</span></li><li><span class="an">1794</span><span class="tx">Adopted under the first name Fougère, the name of the day of his birth in the French Republican calendar.</span></li><li><span class="an">1803</span><span class="tx">Leaves for the United States with a false passport, meant to spare him Napoleonic conscription.</span></li><li><span class="an">1808</span><span class="tx">Marries Lucy Bakewell. The couple set off to try their luck on the Kentucky frontier.</span></li><li><span class="an">1819</span><span class="tx">Bankruptcy, debtors' prison. He has nothing left to live on but his pencil, and takes up portraiture.</span></li><li><span class="an">1826</span><span class="tx">Sails for England with 250 drawings, seeking engravers and subscribers.</span></li><li><span class="an">1827</span><span class="tx">First instalment of <em>The Birds of America</em>. Engraving begins in Edinburgh with Lizars, then moves to London with Havell.</span></li><li><span class="an">1838</span><span class="tx">Final plate. Eleven years of publication, 435 plates, 1,065 birds.</span></li><li><span class="an">1851</span><span class="tx">Dies in Manhattan, on the estate that will become the Audubon Park neighbourhood.</span></li></ul>

  <h3 style="margin-top:78px;font-size:26px">The work</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:14px"><em>The Birds of America</em> is as much a technical
  folly as an artistic one. Audubon insisted that every bird be shown life-size:
  this called for "double elephant" paper, the largest format available, and the
  flamingo had to bend its neck to fit on the page. Every plate was etched and
  aquatinted, then coloured by hand, one at a time.</p>
  <div class="faits"><div class="fait"><b>435</b><span>plates engraved, hand-coloured, published from 1827 to 1838</span></div><div class="fait"><b>1,065</b><span>birds depicted, all life-size</span></div><div class="fait"><b>97 × 66</b><span>centimetres: the "double elephant" format, dictated by the size of the heron</span></div><div class="fait"><b>$11.5</b><span>million, a world record for a printed book, Sotheby's 2010</span></div></div>
  <div class="photos" style="margin-top:44px">
    <figure class="ph tall"><img src="/img/genealogie/audubon-birds-of-america.jpg" alt="The Birds of America" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><em>The Birds of America</em>, the work of a lifetime, published at his own expense over eleven years. <span class="cr">Wikimedia Commons, public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Audubon_Birds_of_America.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="ph tall"><img src="/img/genealogie/the-birds-of-america-livraison-1-plate-i-great-american-cock.jpg" alt="Plate I: the wild turkey" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Plate I: the wild turkey, engraved by W. H. Lizars in Edinburgh. Audubon made the "gobbler" his personal seal, with the motto <em>America my country</em>. <span class="cr">BnF via Wikimedia Commons, public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_birds_of_America._%28Livraison%29_1%2C_Plate_I%2C_Great_American_Cock_Male_%3B_Vulgo_Wild_Turkey_-_estampe_-_Drawn_by_J._J._Audubon_%3B_Engraved_by_W._H._Lizars_Edin.r_-_btv1b53144013v.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="ph tall"><img src="/img/genealogie/the-birds-of-america-livraison-1-plate-v-bonaparte-flycatche.jpg" alt="Plate V: the Canada warbler" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Plate V, first instalment. Every bird is painted life-size, on its host plant. <span class="cr">BnF via Wikimedia Commons, public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_birds_of_America._%28Livraison_1%29%2C_Plate_V%2C_Bonaparte_Flycatcher_%28i.e._Canada_warbler%29_%3B_Plant_seed_pud_-_estampe_-_Drawn_by_J._J._Audubon_%3B_Engraved_by_W._H._Lizars_Edin.r_-_btv1b53143985q.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:36px">Funding came through subscription, plate after plate,
  over eleven years. The undertaking cost Audubon $115,640 of the day's money —
  more than two million today — which he financed by selling oil copies, portraits
  and the skins of animals shot along the way. Fewer than a hundred and twenty
  complete copies survive. In December 2010, one of them sold for $11.5 million at
  Sotheby's in London: the highest price ever paid for a printed book.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:78px;font-size:26px">The one who paid</h3>
  <div class="grid2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div>
      <p>Someone earned those hundred and fifteen thousand dollars. Lucy Bakewell
      was sixteen and Audubon eighteen when he landed in 1803 at Mill Grove, his
      father's property, adjoining Fatland Ford where the Bakewells lived. They
      were neighbours before they were husband and wife; they married on 5 April
      1808.</p>
      <p>After the 1819 bankruptcy, it was she who became the family's
      breadwinner, to free her husband and let him devote himself to the birds.
      She left for Louisiana in 1821 and taught, first for board and lodging, then
      on her own account. From 1823 to 1830, she ran classes for young girls at
      Beech Woods and then at Beech Grove — in that same parish of West Feliciana
      where Audubon painted at Oakley, and where the bridge today bears his name.</p>
      <p>She founded two schools at a time when women had practically no right to
      earn a wage. She obtained an eight-thousand-dollar advance on her
      inheritance from her father, and managed to save three thousand dollars to
      send Audubon to publish his book in Europe. While he was away for months,
      sometimes years, she raised their sons, ran the household's affairs, shipped
      the skins and plants he asked for, kept the subscriptions and coordinated
      the engravers' work.</p>
      <p>Four children, of whom two daughters died in infancy — Lucy in 1817, Rosa
      in 1820. She would outlive her husband and both her sons, and would die in
      1874, aged eighty-seven, in Kentucky.</p>
      <blockquote style="margin-top:26px">
        If I were jealous, I should have a bad time of it, for every bird is my rival.
        <cite>Lucy Bakewell Audubon</cite>
      </blockquote>
    </div>
    <div><figure class="ph haut"><img src="/img/genealogie/mrs-audubon-wife-of-james-audubon.jpg" alt="Portrait of Lucy Bakewell Audubon" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Lucy Bakewell Audubon (1787-1874). Without her, not a single plate would have been engraved. <span class="cr">Wikimedia Commons, public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mrs._Audubon_-_wife_of_James_Audubon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure></div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:78px;font-size:26px">Three anecdotes</h3>
  <div class="grid2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div>
      <p><strong>The silver thread.</strong> In the spring of 1804, at Mill Grove
      in Pennsylvania, Audubon tied a silver thread to the leg of five fledglings —
      eastern phoebes — to find out whether birds return to nest where they were
      born. He claimed to have found two of them again the following year. The
      story earned him the title of America's first bird bander. It is disputed
      today: a 40% return rate is twenty times higher than what modern
      ornithologists observe, and a reconstruction of the dates shows he was not
      in Pennsylvania in the spring of 1805.</p>
      <p><strong>The fish hoax.</strong> In 1818, the naturalist Constantine
      Rafinesque stayed with him in Kentucky. One night, believing he was catching
      a bat of a new species, he smashed his host's favourite violin. Audubon took
      his revenge by describing to him entirely imaginary fish and mammals of the
      Ohio, which Rafinesque seriously published as new species.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p><strong>The eagle that never existed.</strong> His career took off with
      the "Bird of Washington," a giant eagle he claimed to have observed and of
      which he made a spectacular plate. No specimen has ever been found, no
      independent observation ever confirmed it, and recent scholarship concludes
      it was fabricated. The bird that made him famous probably never flew.</p>
      <p>These three episodes take nothing away from the work. They are a
      reminder that Audubon was also an entrepreneur in search of subscribers, in
      a scientific world where discovering a species was worth a reputation.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:78px;font-size:26px">What bears his name</h3>
  <div class="grid2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div>
      <p>A mountain in Colorado, first: the <strong>Mount Audubon</strong>,
      4,032 metres, in the Indian Peaks of the Front Range, north-west of Boulder.
      A Manhattan neighbourhood next, Audubon Park, built on the estate where he
      ended his days. Several birds, finally — Audubon's warbler, Audubon's
      oriole, Audubon's shearwater.</p>
      <p>And above all the <strong>National Audubon Society</strong>, founded in
      1905, more than half a century after his death and with no connection to
      his family. It is today the oldest and largest bird protection organisation
      in the United States.</p>
      <p>Two structures also bear his name, and say a good deal about what he
      became over there. At Henderson, in Kentucky, where he kept a shop from
      1810 to 1819, a museum built in 1938 houses one of the largest collections
      of his originals — in a building in French Norman style raised in tribute
      to his origins, whose tower has niches cut specially for birds to nest in.</p>
      <p>And on the Mississippi, between the parishes of Pointe Coupee and West
      Feliciana, the <strong>John James Audubon Bridge</strong>, opened in May
      2011. Its 482-metre main span made it, on opening, the longest cable-stayed
      bridge in the Western Hemisphere. It replaces a ferry and remains the only
      river crossing for a hundred and fifty kilometres. It spans the river a few
      miles from Oakley Plantation, where Audubon painted some of his birds in
      1821.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p>This name is contested. Audubon owned slaves — nine people worked in his
      household — sold them at his bankruptcy, bought more in the 1820s, and
      publicly opposed abolition. On 15 March 2023, after more than a year of
      consultation, the National Audubon Society's board of directors voted to
      keep the name, judging that the organisation "is bigger than the name of
      one person," while committing $25 million to its diversity programmes.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="photos deux" style="margin-top:40px">
    <figure class="ph wide-img"><img src="/img/genealogie/john-james-audubon-state-park-museum.jpg" alt="The museum at John James Audubon State Park" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>The museum at Henderson, Kentucky, built in 1938 in a French Norman style in tribute to his origins. Its tower is pierced with niches where birds can nest. <span class="cr">Wikimedia Commons · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_James_Audubon_State_Park_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="ph wide-img"><img src="/img/genealogie/john-james-bridge-5.jpg" alt="The John James Audubon Bridge over the Mississippi" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>The John James Audubon Bridge, opened in 2011: a 482-metre main span, the longest cable-stayed bridge in the Western Hemisphere on opening. <span class="cr">Wikimedia Commons · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_James_Bridge_5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:78px;font-size:26px">His descendants</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:14px">His two surviving sons, Victor Gifford and John
  Woodhouse, married the two daughters of John Bachman, the naturalist-pastor of
  Charleston who collaborated on <em>The Viviparous Quadrupeds of North
  America</em>: Maria Rebecca and Mary Eliza, both of whom died before thirty, a
  year apart. Both brothers remarried.</p>
  <p>The American male line then very nearly died out. According to the biographer
  Francis Hobart Herrick, William Bakewell Audubon (1847-1932), the painter's
  grandson, emigrated to Australia; his son Leonard Benjamin, born in 1888,
  remained the sole male representative of the American branch of the Audubons.</p>
</section>
</div>
</div>

<div class="wide">
<section>
  <div class="num-sec">VII · The land today</div>
  <h2>Les Touches</h2>
  <p class="chapo">Two thousand six hundred and thirty-eight inhabitants in 2023,
  canton of Nort-sur-Erdre. The hamlet of Les Mazures still exists. The church of
  Sainte-Mélaine, where Jeanne Rabine was baptised in 1758, was rebuilt in the
  nineteenth century, but the commune keeps alive the memory of the woman who left
  it.</p>
  <div class="photos" style="margin-top:40px">
    <figure class="ph wide-img"><img src="/img/genealogie/panorama-du-mont-juillet-les-touches.jpg" alt="Panorama from Mont-Juillet at Les Touches" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>The bocage seen from Mont-Juillet: the landscape of nine generations out of sixteen. <span class="cr">Wikimedia Commons · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_du_Mont-Juillet_%28Les_Touches%29.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure>
