  <p style="margin-top:30px">The curve declines steadily from the seventeenth
  century to the early nineteenth, with a marked dip for the generation born
  between 1800 and 1849 — the one that reached adulthood in an impoverished
  countryside and faced the cholera epidemics. The twentieth-century recovery is
  spectacular: more than twenty-six years gained in a century.</p>
  <div class="reserve">
    <h3>A caveat on the first point</h3>
    <p>The 61 years shown for the 1550-1599 cohort are a statistical mirage. For
    that period, only people who lived long — and so left several records — could
    be identified. Infants who died without a recorded baptism appear nowhere.
    The figure measures the survival of documents, not of people.</p>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:30px"><strong>Eleven per cent of the dated individuals die
  before the age of five.</strong> The real figure was far higher: those are
  precisely the children the registers forget most.</p>
</section>
</div>

<div class="wide">
<section>
  <div class="num-sec">XI · The sources</div>
  <h2>The file, and a filmed archive</h2>
  <p class="chapo">Everything above comes from a genealogical database that
  anyone can take up, verify and correct. It is made available in GEDCOM format,
  the standard exchange format in genealogy, readable by every piece of software
  in the field.</p>

  <div class="grid2" style="margin-top:40px">
    <div>
      <p><a href="/genealogie-bonnet-public.ged" download><strong>Download the GEDCOM file</strong></a>
      — 2,169 individuals, 668 families, 1.8 MB.
      <span class="mono" style="display:block;font-size:12.5px;color:var(--ardoise-7);margin-top:6px">Version with living persons redacted.</span></p>
      <p>This version has been corrected and deduplicated: broken encoding
      fixed, dates normalised, commune names harmonised, eighty-two duplicate
      records merged. People born less than a hundred years ago whose death is
      not established appear here under the name "Living," with no date or place
      — a publication convention that protects the living while preserving the
      structure of the tree. Links to family photographs have been removed.</p>
      <p style="font-size:16px;color:var(--ardoise-5)">Any remaining errors are
      flagged as such in the pages above. Any correction backed by a record is
      welcome.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 style="font-size:20px;font-weight:600">Filmed archive</h3>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><a href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1NXbqo0ZiaKAyF2x33D9cgGWwatRBPlpw/view" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View the filmed archive on Google Drive</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<div class="pied">
  <div class="wide">
    <div class="fgrid">
      <div>
        <h3>Method</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Database of 2,169 individuals and 668 families, from a MyHeritage export, corrected and deduplicated</li>
          <li>Cross-checked against an independent 2020 GeneWeb database, which arrives at the same common ancestor by a different path</li>
          <li>Every figure cited comes from a direct count on the file and remains recalculable</li>
          <li>People born less than a hundred years ago whose death is not established are not named</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Sources</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Archives départementales de Loire-Atlantique, parish registers of Les Touches, Mésanger, Riaillé, Ligné</li>
          <li>Commune of Les Touches, note "Jean-Jacques Audubon, naturaliste"</li>
          <li>F. H. Herrick, <em>Audubon the Naturalist</em>, 1917</li>
          <li>Y. Chatelain, <em>Audubon, peintre, naturaliste, aventurier</em>, 2001</li>
          <li>M. J. Halley, "Audubon's famous banding experiment: fact or fiction?", <em>Archives of Natural History</em>, 2018</li>
          <li>National Audubon Society, open letter of 16 March 2023</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Illustrations</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Plates from <em>The Birds of America</em> and portraits: public domain, via Wikimedia Commons and the Library of Congress</li>
          <li>Views of Les Touches: Wikimedia Commons</li>
          <li>Charts and maps: produced for this report from the genealogical data</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:46px;font-size:13px;color:var(--ardoise-8);max-width:none">
      Investigation compiled in August 2026. The older filiations rest on
      transcriptions of parish records and remain, like any genealogy,
      susceptible to correction by a return to the registers.</p>
  </div>
</div>

